<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P19</oops:Code><oops:Name>Defining multiple domains or ranges in properties</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>The domain or range (or both) of a property (relationships and attributes) is defined by stating more than one rdfs:domain or rdfs:range statements. In OWL multiple rdfs:domain or rdfs:range axioms are allowed, but they are interpreted as conjunction, being, therefore, equivalent to the construct owl:intersectionOf. This pitfall is related to the common error that appears when defining domains and ranges described in [7].	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Critical</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>6</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.org/net/p-plan#isPrecededBy</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.org/mp#challenges</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://example.org/whu#hasGoal</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.org/mp#supports</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_is_about</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P22</oops:Code><oops:Name>Using different naming conventions in the ontology</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>The ontology elements are not named following the same convention (for example CamelCase or use of delimiters as &quot;-&quot; or &quot;_&quot;) . Some notions about naming conventions are provided in [2].	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Minor</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>1</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000100</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityValue</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P32</oops:Code><oops:Name>Several classes with the same label</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>Two or more classes have the same content for natural language annotations for naming, for example the rdfs:label annotation. This pitfall might involve lack of accuracy when defining terms.	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Minor</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>2</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:HaveSameLabel>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://example.org/whu#DataItem</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000027</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:HaveSameLabel>
<oops:HaveSameLabel>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://example.org/whu#DataSet</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000100</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:HaveSameLabel>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P34</oops:Code><oops:Name>Untyped class</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>An ontology element is used as a class without having been explicitly declared as such using the primitives owl:Class or rdfs:Class. This pitfall is related with the common problems listed in [8].	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Important</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>2</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#Dataset</oops:AffectedElement>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#DataService</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Code>P35</oops:Code><oops:Name>Untyped property</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>An ontology element is used as a property without having been explicitly declared as such using the primitives rdf:Property, owl:ObjectProperty or owl:DatatypeProperty. This pitfall is related with the common problems listed in [8].	</oops:Description>
<oops:Importance>Important</oops:Importance>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>1</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:Affects>
</oops:Pitfall>
<oops:Suggestion>
<oops:Name>SUGGESTION: symmetric or transitive object properties.</oops:Name>
<oops:Description>The domain and range axioms are equal for each of the following object properties. Could they be symmetric or transitive?</oops:Description>
<oops:NumberAffectedElements>1</oops:NumberAffectedElements>
<oops:Affects>
<oops:AffectedElement>http://purl.org/net/p-plan#isPrecededBy</oops:AffectedElement>
</oops:Affects>
